\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We reformulated execution simulation for code repair verification from open-ended output generation to per-test binary classification. Our central finding is that the advantage of this reformulation is complexity-dependent: on function-level code with short, structurally simple outputs, classification and generation achieve near-equivalent accuracy; at repository scale, classification dominates by a wide margin. The generation formulation suffers a compounding failure on complex code---format non-compliance discards the majority of predictions, and the surviving parseable outputs are themselves unreliable, falling below chance-level accuracy (50\%). Classification is immune to this failure mode by construction, as binary verdicts require no structured output parsing. Moreover, at function level, scaling model capacity does not resolve the generation bottleneck: \cwm{} accuracy decreases by 0.27\pp{} from 4B to 8B, consistent with a formulation-level bottleneck at this complexity tier. Independently, per-test classification provides fine-grained diagnostic signals---identifying which specific tests a patch fails---at equivalent trajectory-level accuracy, with the advantage concentrating in instances that contain multiple fail-to-pass tests.

For test-time scaling pipelines evaluating patches against multi-test suites, classification avoids the compounding failure that degrades generation on complex code. Per-test verdicts unlock targeted re-repair guided by failure localization rather than binary accept/reject decisions.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
All experiments use a single model family (Qwen3); scaling behavior may differ across architectures or pre-training regimes. The repo-level same-size comparison rests on a single seed; the second seed's repo-level comparison is cross-size (8B \cls{} vs.\ 4B \cwm{}), not an apple-to-apple same-size match. The 8B--8B comparison awaits \cwm{} 8B training completion. No downstream re-repair experiment validates whether per-test diagnostic signals translate to actual fix improvements. Pass-to-pass regression rates are negligible (1.04\% mean, 0\% median), meaning the per-test advantage currently concentrates in fail-to-pass discrimination; regression detection---a potential benefit of per-test granularity---remains untested in practice. Training data comprises synthetic mutations; transfer to naturally occurring developer bugs is untested. A matched-context ablation (\cls{} evaluated at \texttt{max\_seq\_length}$=$2,048) confirms that $>$95\% of the \cls{}--\cwm{} gap stems from formulation differences, not input context disparity. The oracle analysis assumes ground-truth test labels; real-world per-test advantage is upper-bounded by classifier accuracy.

\paragraph{Future work.}
\cls{}--\cwm{} error complementarity (oracle: 90.63\%) motivates ensemble verification that routes instances by predicted output complexity---using generation when outputs are simple and classification when they are complex. Per-test failure signals enable diagnostic re-repair: localizing faults from per-test verdicts rather than discarding failed patches outright. Multi-model validation across architectures, completion of the 8B--8B repo-level comparison, and integration with test-time scaling systems (best-of-$N$ with per-test reranking) are natural next steps.

